theory:
## Swapping Two Values

Swapping the contents of two variables is surprisingly common. XIOM provides `mem.swap` to do it elegantly:

```xiom
use xiom.mem;

fn main() -> Int {
  var a = 10;
  var b = 20;

  mem.swap(&mut a, &mut b);

  // Now a is 20, b is 10!
  return a;
}
```

### What is &mut?

The `&mut` means "mutable reference" — it gives `mem.swap` permission to modify your variables. Don't worry about the details now; you'll learn about references in Level 3.

### Why Not Do It Manually?

```xiom
// Manual swap — needs a temporary variable
var a = 10;
var b = 20;

var temp = a;  // Save a
var a_then = b;  // This won't work — we need the same variable!
// Actually, manual swap:
var temp_val = a;
a = b;
b = temp_val;  // Now a=20, b=10
```

```xiom
// With mem.swap — one line, no temp variable
mem.swap(&mut a, &mut b);
```

### Common Uses

```xiom
use xiom.mem;

fn main() -> Int {
  // Sorting two numbers
  var first = 50;
  var second = 30;

  if first > second {
    mem.swap(&mut first, &mut second);
  }
  // Now first <= second

  // Swapping positions
  var current = 1;
  var next = 2;
  mem.swap(&mut current, &mut next);

  return 0;
}
```

### The Pattern

```xiom
use xiom.mem;

// Always: mem.swap(&mut x, &mut y)
// Both variables must be var (mutable)
// Both must be the same type
// After swap: x has y's old value, y has x's old value
```

### When to Swap

- Sorting algorithms (bubble sort, selection sort)
- Shuffling (randomizing order)
- Alternating between two configurations
- Exchanging buffer contents

